          'Tensors: multi-dimensional arrays of numbers (generalizing scalars, vectors, and matrices) that hold data, weights, and activations; every operation in a deep learning model is expressed as tensor math, so shape and dtype bugs are among the most common engineering errors.',
          'Forward pass: the sequence of tensor operations that turns an input into a model output (and a loss, during training); understanding it precisely is required before backpropagation makes sense, since backpropagation differentiates exactly this sequence.',
          'Backpropagation: the reverse-mode application of the chain rule through the computation graph built during the forward pass, producing the gradient of the loss with respect to every trainable parameter; it is the mechanism that makes large-scale neural network training computationally feasible.',
          'Activation functions: nonlinear functions (ReLU, GELU, sigmoid, etc.) applied between layers; without them, stacking linear layers would collapse into a single linear function, so activations are what give deep networks the capacity to model complex patterns.',
